<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Shaddadids of Ani</h1><p class="st-meta">1072 CE – 1199 CE · capital Ani</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Kurdish Muslim dynasty that governed the old Armenian capital Ani for the Seljuks, alternating with Georgian conquerors for over a century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a> took Ani in 1064, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-shah-i">Malik Shah</a> granted the city in 1072 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihr-ibn-shavur">Manuchihr</a>, son of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shaddadid-emirate">Shaddadid Emirate</a>'s ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-aswar-shavur-i">Shavur</a>. The branch at Ani outlived the main line in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> deposed in 1075.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> governed a largely Armenian Christian city, maintaining its walls and trade and treating the population with some tolerance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihr-ibn-shavur">Manuchihr</a> built the mosque that still stands at Ani, among the earliest Turkic-era mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a> repeatedly captured the city, in 1124, 1161 and 1174, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shaddadid-emirate">Shaddadids</a> regained it each time.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1199 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamar-of-georgia">Queen Tamar's</a> generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakare-mkhargrdzeli">Zakare</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivane-mkhargrdzeli">Ivane</a> took Ani for good and made it the centre of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zakarid-armenia">Zakarid Armenia</a>, ending Shaddadid rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuchihr-ibn-shavur">Manuchihr</a> mosque, later used as a museum, remains one of the few standing buildings in the ruins of Ani.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1072 CE – 1199 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.03%;width:2.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1072 CE – 1199 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ani</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manuchihr, Shahanshah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian; Kurdish; Armenian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>, <a href="/countries/armenia">Armenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Ani, Shaddadid Ani</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1136&amp;focus=shaddadids-of-ani" aria-label="Shaddadids of Ani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5749 730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shaddadids of Ani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6069 986L6136 981M6069 986L6481 1127M6069 986L6036 930"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6136 981h0M6481 1127h0M6036 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6069 986h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6069 986h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ani. <a href="/atlas?year=1136&amp;focus=shaddadids-of-ani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
